d to support  any new development  and ultimately decisions will not be  allowed on developments that would cause  unacceptable harm to the traffic network  and less mitigation can be secured  i would also emphasize the council work  closely with the nhs and developments  are required to contribute financially  to the nhs to allow local facilities to  be expanded so this includes primary  care uh to south bend hospital how that  money is actually spent though is a  responsibility for the local health  trusts  so the next question that we have is  why is road infrastructure not at the  top of the list to be tackled first  so again road infrastructure is a key  consideration in any future decisions  about the new local plan strategy so  that transport assessment i mentioned  previously we will consider the impact  of attention to development on traffic  flows on key roads and junctions and we  will be working with business county  council to try to make sure that  investments can be made to the road  network to make sure that it is as  sustainable as possible  clearly if developments are going to  have an unacceptable impact on the road  network that can't be mitigated they are  unlikely to be selected as part of the  new local plan  so the next question is will the  boundaries of the roach valley be taken  into consideration so  the boundaries of the upper roach valley  special landscape area which is an area  of broad land  from around southeast rayleigh  under to the south of of hockley and out  towards  rochford in the east  is an identified constraint it's an area  that's identified as being of importance  for  landscaping it's also an area that's  identified as a kind of green lung for  the district so all of that those  qualities will be taken into account  when considering potential sites for  development there is a landscape  character assessment that the council  has had prepared  which is available on the consultation  website and that again reiterates the  importance of that area for its  landscape qualities  so the next question is  will you be preventing the use of  already dwindling green spaces in the  district for building  so green spaces in the sense of parks  and open spaces are not currently being  considered for development as part of  the new local plan  in fact the consultation document  identifies a whole wide range of these  open spaces both public open spaces  private open spaces and  recommends them for  protection there's also a specific  policy  looked at in the consultation document  known as local green space and those are  green spaces that are particularly  important to local communities  and through local plans councils can  identify local green spaces that are  important to the local communities and  afford them the same strength of  protection from development as greenbelt  land  so for green space is in a wider sense  it is worth reiterating again that  council and government policy is very  clear that brownfield and vacant sites  need to be developed prior to  considering greenfield sites for  development  so the next question that we received  was  what steps are the council planning to  address  uh planning to address housing in this  district for the hidden homeless those  sofa surfing and adults forced to live  with parents as they have low wages and  the price to rent slash buy is too high  so i would advise that around a third of  home build on larger sites are made  available for affordable housing and the  council has 100 nomination rights on the  initial way for these properties and a  local eligibility test  it is however also working out that  we're pointing out that a new type of  affordable housing has recently been  introduced by government and this is  called first homes this is specifically  a type of affordable housing that is  made available to first-time buyers and  that's made available to first-time  buyers in perpetuity so if you as a  first-time buyer were to buy that house  you would then need to sell it to  another first-time buyer and maintain a  percentage discount for our in  perpetuity  uh so through the local plan the council  can obviously try to make sure as much  of those affordable homes are developed  as possible uh and make sure that policy  requirement on  new development sites uh pushes that  percentage uh as high as it can go so at  the moment like i said around a third  that it may be possible to require 40 or  even higher than 40  of homes to be different types of  affordable tenure and that obviously  includes those first homes but also  social housing uh affordable rent and  other types of affordable housing  uh it's also probably worth pointing out  that generally trying to address housing  issues is also important and comes back  to some of those strategy issues  mentioned earlier in the presentation  about how housing need could be  addressed in a more general sense  so the next question that has been asked  is  what provisions do the council have to  accommodate the disabled members of our  society so access to some areas are  restricted for wheelchair users woodland  walks pavements and roads  vehicles cars lorries vans use the  pavements as parking and cyclists use  them as their very own road as they feel  the actual roads are too dangerous for  them making it hazardous for those with  visual impairment severe visual  impairment wheelchairs etc  so the council can create policies in  its new local plan that require  developments to be built to suitable  standards of accessibility  it is more challenging to retrofit  existing  services and existing areas um but it  may be possible for the new local plan  to help fund accessibility improvements  in certain locations so development's  being built in a village may be able to  make contributions to improve the  accessibility of local parks or local  woodland local pavements etc as part of  its kind of community contribution  through um through section 106's which i  mentioned earlier uh also currently  three percent of homes the largest sites  are required to be built to full  wheelchair accessibility standards again  through the new local plan it may be  possible to review this and revise this  figure upwards to make sure more homes  that are being built on development  sites are ready made for those with uh  movement impairment  or those using wheelchairs  so the next uh the next question we have  received is  how many applicants are currently on  rdc's housing waiting list and what  percentage of the proposed additional  housing will be social or affordable  housing  so i can advise there are currently  around 980 individuals on the council's  housing register  and as previously stated the council's  current policies require around a third  of new homes on larger sites to be one  of those affordable tenures such as  first homes affordable rent social  housing etc so the current split of  affordable housing that comes out of  that third is around 80 affordable rent  and about 20 shared ownership so shared  ownership is a housing model where an  individual can buy a percentage of a  house and then rent the remaining  percentage uh from a housing association  in some cases they may be able to  ultimately buy the housing association  stake in other cases  they would rent in perpetuity and move  uh potentially move to an alternative  house at a certain point  so ultimately this policy can be  reviewed and the requirements um around  affordable housing could be increased so  that could include both the number of  affordable homes expected on new  developments so that 33 or 35 figure  could be increased to 40 or uh even  higher percentage uh and also that's  split um of different types of  affordable housing could also be  reviewed um to make sure that it's an  appropriate mix of affordable housing to  meet the needs of local people there is  also an opportunity to  to be more clear about the types of  housing in terms of size that should be  provided alongside affordable housing  provision  um so just um  just to answer the second part of the  question what percentage of the proposed  additional housing will be social  affordable housing well based on the  current policy you might expect a third  of those new homes to be affordable um  however obviously as i've said it may be  a higher figure ultimately  so the next question we've received is  why have sites identified as having an  adverse impact on heritage been included  within the promoted sites  so it's worth saying again that the  consultation document includes all of  the sites that landowners have asked us  to consider so as i said in the answer  to a previous question the council  issued what's known as a call for sites  and this call for sites gave landowners  and developers the opportunity to  formally ask the council to consider  particular pieces of land when it was  putting forward its  local plan  so at this stage we have not sought to  sift sites from that list and therefore  we we are very aware there will be sites  within uh that list of uh promoted sites  that actually are subject to quite  significant constraints and that's not  just heritage that might also include  blood risk it might also include uh  protected habitats and some more  information um on um the the initial  site appraisal that the council has done  on each of these sites is available if  using the interactive map  mentioned earlier  and you may be able to see through that  scoring process exactly how different  sites have been assessed and it may be  that it that it's quite clear in that  assessment process that actually certain  issues to do with sites have been scored  um  and they show up as a very poor score  against that criteria and again those  types of issues will all be taken into  account when decisions are made about  ultimately which sites need to be  developed  so  the next question that has been asked  is  ultimately where does the demand for new  houses come from  um  so it's worth saying again that national  government policy does require local  councils to plan to meet their housing  needs and the starting point for any  council's local plan is the government  housing need methodology and again that  results in a figure as i've stated  previously of around 360 homes per year  for rochford  but the demand for new houses comes from  various sources um feedback from recent  developments suggests that actually a  majority of the homes being reserved on  new development sites  are being  sold to local people including people  mostly with an ss postcode for example  so rocksford has known issues with  concealed households which are  basically um homes that are  are being lived in by too many people um  and ideally uh you know a subset of  those people would like to move into  their own home  and we have also got known issues in  terms of housing affordability and how  that affects first-time buyers but also  others in the community  so all of those things do contribute to  quite a lot  of  uh housing demand  in the area that's not currently being  met but might not be completely obvious  because those people do have homes it's  just that those homes don't really meet  their needs  however that you know some percentage of  the demand for new housing does come  from people from outside the area  however this is true of all places and  indeed rocha residents are free to also  move to other parts of the area  and and so this is inevitable um  to a large extent  so the next question that has been asked  is uh will the field at the back of  hopley community center owned by odyssey  be allowed to be used as a road for  access to enabling development on the  field belonging to vets farm once its  leased commune center ends so there are  no plans for this as  part of this consultation  so that that isn't being considered as  an option as part of this consultation  so the next question is  how can the council justify the amount  of roadworks on vital roads happening  simultaneously  so it's worth just pointing out the  permission for road works does not fall  under richard rosh council but the issue  has been noted previously  and brought to us county council's  attention um so obviously there's a hope  that that issue will not be repeated um  but it doesn't unfortunately fall under  watchful district council direct direct  implements  so that actually takes us to the end of  the questions that have been submitted  to us there  may be some questions um that haven't  been answered on this session because  they came in after the presentation was  prepared or perhaps because the answer  to them was similar to the answer to  another question so i would just  reiterate at this point that all the  questions submitted through the online  form will receive a written response on  the council's website in due course  and therefore if you haven't seen the  exact question that you want asked  answered in this session it will be  answered  through that written document  so just wrapping up um  there are obviously a few things that um  we would like people to take away from  from this webinar obviously one of which  is is knowing better how to access the  consultation  so  just to reiterate again uh there are  three main ways that people can access  the consultation so those are to use the  online consultation portal which is  available at rochester.oc2.uk  and clicking on that top link that says  new local plan spatial options document  2021  if you go by that way you need to  navigate through the document and find  purple question boxes with blue speech  bubbles next to them if you click on the  blue speech bubble you will be prompted  to leave a comment you may need to log  in or create a profile before you can do  that  the second way of accessing the  consultation is using downloadable  copies that you can download straight to  your computer again to access these you  can go to rochford.oc2.uk  you need to click on the black bar that  says spatial options downloadable copies  and topic papers you need to click from  the two links that that are described as  either the stylized version or the  simple version just to emphasize again  the simple version may display better on  mobile devices or if you plan to print  the document if you access document in  that way you can either submit comments  via email to spatial options at  rochefort.gov  or by post to council offices south  street rochford ss41bw  the third and final way you can access  the consultation is by visiting a local  library or council offices when open and  viewing a physical copy of the document  there will be paper response forms that  you can return to the council  alternatively you can also email  comments in to spatial options at  ruptured.gov dot uk  we'll just emphasize again the point  about the background material so this is  again all linked from rochford.oc2.uk  and you can find information about the  technical studies which are the  evidence-based you can find information  about the topic papers which are the  non-technical summaries you can also  find information about the interactive  map if you have any difficulty using the  interactive map we have published a  guide on the website  uh  written answers to each of the questions  um put forward for the question form as  i said earlier we'll be publishing the  council's website shortly i would just  also emphasize at this point that  you're not obliged to respond to every  question in the consultation document  and you're not obliged to respond  to any of the questions if you feel that  your comments do not neatly fit under  any of those questions you are able just  to email or write in your general views  and then we will manually assign that to  the document if that works easier for  people then that then that is acceptable  too  the consultation closes at 5pm on  wednesday the 22nd of september so again  this is a two-week extension from that  previously advertised  um  and lastly if there are any difficulties  accessing the consultation or further  questions  you can send these to planning.policy at  rockford.gov the uk  so um that is different to the spatial  options at rochford.gov uk email address  which is for  formal comments on the consultation it  would be better to use planning.policy  at rockford.gov uk if you have any  questions about how to access or use the  consultation  so thank you for listening to this  webinar um i would i would thank you for  your time  ultimately the consultation is an  opportunity for people to help shape the  future stages of the local plan we are  still at an early stage and therefore no  decisions have been made about the  contents of that local plan however with  each stage as it progresses obviously  more detail will be published and  ultimately in 2023 we hope to have a  final local plan ready to submit to  government um so it is important people  get involved at every stage um so that  they can be comfortable that the  direction that that local plan heads in  um  takes account of their views  so thank you for your time uh this  evening um  ultimately if you do need to contact our  team please do so before the  consultation deadline um and i hope to  see you very soon thank you

